  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/e64215da53594d7e7bc24f8df953aa84f0648cd3ab58d0a8b8005be653c28902.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$ \n {{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061|\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{(-3)}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the second quadrant. So, we find the angle in the second quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$",
